---
/**
 * Starlight override: the theme is dark, and there is nothing to provide.
 *
 * Starlight's own `ThemeProvider` reads `localStorage['starlight-theme']`,
 * falls back to `prefers-color-scheme`, and writes `data-theme` on <html>.
 * That is exactly the behaviour being removed: the site is dark everywhere by
 * decision, and a reader whose OS reports light was getting light
 * documentation.
 *
 * This is an override rather than a CSS patch on purpose. Starlight's palette
 * lives in `:root` (dark) with a `:root[data-theme='light']` block on top, so
 * forcing dark in the stylesheet would mean out-shouting every one of those
 * declarations and would break the moment the theme adds one. Removing the
 * thing that WRITES the attribute leaves Starlight's own default — dark —
 * standing, which is the same result with none of the specificity war.
 *
 * The attribute is still written explicitly, for two reasons. A visitor who
 * used the toggle before it was removed has `light` sitting in localStorage;
 * nothing reads it now, but stating `dark` makes that unambiguous rather than
 * merely unread. And `custom.css` recolours Mermaid through tokens that key
 * off this attribute, so it should say what it means.
 *
 * `updatePickers` is kept as a no-op. Starlight calls it from `ThemeSelect`,
 * which this site also overrides to render nothing — so nothing calls it
 * today. It costs one line and it means a future Starlight that calls the
 * global from somewhere else fails silently instead of throwing a
 * ReferenceError over the whole page.
 */
---

{/* Inlined to avoid a flash of the wrong theme, as Starlight's own is. */}
<script is:inline>
  document.documentElement.dataset.theme = 'dark';
  window.StarlightThemeProvider = { updatePickers() {} };
</script>
